[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Modern C# Features in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Recent versions of C# have introduced powerful language features designed to make code more expressive, safer, and easier to maintain. This tutorial explores four core modern features: Records, Init-only properties, Nullable Reference Types, and advanced Pattern Matching."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Records and Init-only Properties"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Records provide a built-in syntax for defining reference types with value-based equality semantics. Combined with init-only properties (using 'init' instead of 'set'), they enable immutable data models that are safer to share across threads."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public record User(string FirstName, string LastName);

// Or with explicit init properties:
public class Product
{
    public int Id { get; init; }
    public string Name { get; init; } = string.Empty;
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Nullable Reference Types (NRT)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Introduced to help eliminate the infamous NullReferenceException, Nullable Reference Types allow you to explicitly state whether a reference type can be null using the '?' suffix. Enable this in your .NET project via <Nullable>enable</Nullable> in the csproj file."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public class UserService
{
    // Non-nullable: must be initialized
    public string Name { get; set; } = "Default";

    // Nullable: explicitly allowed to be null
    public string? MiddleName { get; set; }

    public void PrintName()
    {
        // Compiler warns if accessed without safety checks
        Console.WriteLine(MiddleName?.Length);
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Advanced Pattern Matching"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Pattern matching allows you to inspect data structures and execute logic based on shapes, types, and values. Switch expressions and property patterns drastically reduce boilerplate code."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public decimal CalculateDiscount(object item) => item switch
{
    Book { Price: > 100.0m } => 0.20m,
    Book b => 0.10m,
    Clothing c when c.IsOnSale => 0.50m,
    _ => 0.0m
};"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use records for DTOs, value objects, and immutable data transfer."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enable Nullable Reference Types on all new .NET projects to catch null bugs at compile time."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage switch expressions instead of long if-else chains for cleaner type-driven logic."}]
